." rmdir(1)
.TH RMDIR 1 "April 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rmdir \- remove empty directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rmdir
.RI [ OPTION ]... " DIRECTORY" ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rmdir
removes each named empty
.IR DIRECTORY .
Unlike
.BR rm (1) ,
it does not remove non-directory operands and does not recurse through directory contents.
.PP
Substrate
.B rmdir
validates the target with
.BR fstatat (2)
using
.BR AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW ,
opens parent directories with
.BR openat (2) ,
and performs removal with
.BR unlinkat (2)
and
.BR AT_REMOVEDIR .
Symbolic links are therefore never followed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -p , " --parents"
After removing each operand, attempt to remove its ancestors while they remain empty.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Output a diagnostic for each directory removed:
.PP
.RS
rmdir: removing directory, 'path'
.RE
.TP
.B --ignore-fail-on-non-empty
Treat failures caused by non-empty directories as a stop condition instead of an error.
This affects both the target operand and ancestor removal under
.BR -p .
.TP
.B --help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Display version information and exit.
.SH SAFETY
.B rmdir
refuses to remove
.BR / ,
.B .
and
.BR .. .
.PP
Targets are validated with
.B AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
before removal, so a symbolic link to a directory is rejected with an error instead of being followed.
.SH ANCESTOR REMOVAL
With
.BR -p ,
.B rmdir
removes the named directory first and then walks upward through its textual path, stopping before
.BR / ,
.B .
or
.BR .. .
Removal stops at the first ancestor that cannot be removed.
.PP
Without
.BR --ignore-fail-on-non-empty ,
that failure is reported and the command exits nonzero.
With
.BR --ignore-fail-on-non-empty ,
non-empty ancestors stop the walk quietly while earlier removals remain successful.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
All requested removals succeeded, or stopped only because
.B --ignore-fail-on-non-empty
suppressed a non-empty directory failure.
.TP
1
At least one removal failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
rmdir emptydir
Remove one empty directory.
.TP
rmdir -p a/b/c
Remove
.B c
and then remove
.B b
and
.B a
if they also become empty.
.TP
rmdir --ignore-fail-on-non-empty -p a/b/c
Remove the deepest directory and stop quietly when an ancestor is still populated.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR openat (2),
.BR fstatat (2),
.BR unlinkat (2),
.BR rmdir (2)